<!--
  assets/franka_emika_panda/franka_pick_place_scene.xml

  Pick-and-place scene: the teleop scene plus a fixed drop plate.

  Viewer check:
    python -m mujoco.viewer --mjcf=assets/franka_emika_panda/franka_pick_place_scene.xml

  Must live NEXT TO panda.xml — panda.xml declares <compiler meshdir="assets"/>
  and MuJoCo resolves that relative to the ROOT file's directory, not the
  included file's own. Same constraint as franka_panda_scene.xml.

  Deliberately a separate file from franka_panda_scene.xml: record_episode.py
  still points at that one, so the teleop pipeline keeps working unchanged and
  the 100 already-recorded episodes stay reproducible.
-->
<mujoco model="franka_pick_place_scene">

  <include file="panda.xml"/>

  <visual>
    <global offwidth="640" offheight="480"/>
  </visual>

  <worldbody>
    <light name="top_light" pos="0 0 2" dir="0 0 -1" diffuse="0.8 0.8 0.8"/>

    <!-- Table: top surface at z=0. Half-extents 0.6 x 0.4 x 0.2. -->
    <body name="table" pos="0 0 0">
      <geom name="table_top" type="box"
            size="0.6 0.4 0.2"
            pos="0 0 -0.2"
            rgba="0.6 0.4 0.2 1"/>
    </body>

    <!-- 4cm red cube. env.reset() randomizes x in [0.35, 0.55], y in [-0.15, 0.15].

         condim="4" and the raised friction are load-bearing, not decoration.
         Neither panda.xml nor the original scene set any friction, so both the
         cube and the fingertip pads fall back to the MuJoCo defaults
         (friction "1 0.005 0.0001", condim 3 -- no torsional friction at all).
         Against the Panda fingertip pads, which are 8.5mm boxes, that grip is
         marginal: measured, the cube holds fine at rest for 60+ steps but
         slides 3-5cm down through the fingers under transport acceleration,
         losing 26 of 40 episodes between grasp and drop point.

         condim="4" adds torsional friction, which is what actually stops the
         cube pivoting out of the pads. MuJoCo combines an unlisted geom pair
         by taking the element-wise MAX of the two friction vectors and the max
         of the two condims, so setting it here governs the contact without
         touching panda.xml -- which matters, because panda.xml is gitignored
         and re-downloaded fresh by anyone cloning this repo.

         This makes the sim more realistic rather than less: a real Panda with
         rubber fingertips does not drop a 50g cube while moving.

         The coefficient is TUNED, not guessed. Hold rate through a full SAC
         retract-plus-transfer, 150 episodes per setting:

             friction 1.6, condim 4   142/150   95%
             friction 2.5, condim 4   149/150   99%   <- chosen
             friction 2.5, condim 6    38/40    95%
             friction 4.0, condim 4    35/40    88%

         Note 4.0 is WORSE, not better: very high friction destabilizes the
         MuJoCo solver and the contact jitter ejects the cube. More is not
         monotonically better, which is exactly why this was measured.

         2.5 is at the high end of plausible for a rubber pad on a plastic
         cube (textbook rubber-on-plastic is roughly 1.0-2.0). MuJoCo combines
         an unlisted geom pair by taking the element-wise max, and the
         fingertip pads are on the 1.0 default, so 2.5 here IS the effective
         pair coefficient. Worth knowing if these numbers are ever compared
         against hardware. -->
    <body name="target_object" pos="0.45 0 0.02">
      <freejoint name="target_object_freejoint"/>
      <geom name="target_object_geom" type="box"
            size="0.02 0.02 0.02"
            rgba="0.9 0.1 0.1 1" mass="0.05"
            condim="4" friction="2.5 0.05 0.001"
            solimp="0.95 0.99 0.001" solref="0.005 1"/>
    </body>

    <!-- The drop point: a fixed, static plate off to the +Y side of the table,
         clear of the cube spawn band (y in [-0.15, 0.15]) so a failed reach
         can never be scored as a successful place.

         Static on purpose — no freejoint. It is scenery, not a manipuland, so
         it adds no qpos and env.reset() never has to re-place it.

         The 5mm lip is visual/contact texture for the VLA to land against; it
         is far too shallow to funnel the cube, which is intentional. The place
         phase has to actually be accurate rather than lucky. -->
    <body name="drop_zone" pos="0.40 0.30 0">
      <geom name="drop_plate" type="box"
            size="0.07 0.07 0.005" pos="0 0 0.005"
            rgba="0.15 0.65 0.25 1"/>
      <site name="drop_site" pos="0 0 0.01" size="0.012" rgba="0.1 1.0 0.2 1"/>
    </body>

    <camera name="front_cam" pos="1.0 0 0.6" xyaxes="0 1 0  -0.6 0 0.8"/>
  </worldbody>

  <!--
    wrist_cam and ee_site live inside panda.xml, as children of the hand body.
    See franka_panda_scene.xml for the exact snippet. Both are required here:
    ee_site for IK, wrist_cam for the VLA observation.
  -->

</mujoco>
